From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 09:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-snd: name stuck control steps and warm up playback

STEAMAC-DA (M1, macOS 27.2.1): the guest logs "virtio_snd: control
message (0x00000102) timeout" from 4 s after boot and then every ~6 s for
minutes, without sound; launcher.log has no host-side virtio-snd line, so
it is unknown which CoreAudio call PCM_PREPARE is blocked in.

A watchdog thread now follows each control request on the virtio-snd
worker. A request still running after the guest's 1 s timeout is logged
once at warn while still blocked, with the host step it is in: the stream
locks, output device lookup, AudioComponentInstanceNew/CurrentDevice,
StreamFormat/ChannelMap, AudioUnitInitialize, AudioOutputUnitStart/Stop,
latency query. A request that took over 250 ms logs its total and slowest
step when it finishes. Only the request thread's steps count; the pump,
capture and warm-up threads running the same setup do not relabel it.

The watchdog's first end-to-end run on the development M1 Max found a cold
CoreAudio: the first PCM_PREPARE spent 2069 ms in AudioComponentInstanceNew/
CurrentDevice, and the guest timed out PREPARE and the SET_PARAMS behind it
(as in the earlier boot-time timeout here). The playback output unit is
now created by a warm-up thread when the backend starts, while the guest
boots; PREPARE finds it (or waits on the stream lock while it is created).
Its first configure rechecks the default output route, since route changes
are only followed once a format exists.

Tests: tests/macos_control_watchdog.rs checks a blocked request is reported
while blocked and once, names its step and not another thread's, and fast
requests log nothing. A throwaway VM with a 1.5 s injected PREPARE delay
logged the stuck step and the completion; without it, the warm-up unit was
used by the first PREPARE (34 ms, sound playing, no guest timeouts). The
cold-CoreAudio case itself cannot be forced; the persistent DA timeouts are
not explained by it alone.
